// EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR E_TYPE_NOT_IN_SET
//
// Registry #941, the ARGUMENT half — a constructor call written directly as an
// argument must be judged by the same rule as the same value written through a
// binding.
//
// Until 2026-09-05 it was not. `only_ints(Meters(2.5))` compiled clean while
//
//     ro m Meters = Meters(2.5)
//     only_ints(m)
//
// was refused — one value, two spellings, two answers. The membership check
// (D310) never ran for the first form because `call_return_ty` looked the callee
// up in `fn_decls` only: `Meters` is a call by syntax and a CONSTRUCTOR by
// meaning, so it lives in `type_decls` and the lookup missed. `None` then reads
// as "not inferable — best-effort skip".
//
// WHY A NEWTYPE OVER `f64` AND NOT OVER `int`: the carrier has to be a type whose
// exclusion from `Ints` is not arguable. D310 says membership is by explicit
// member list — `~underlying`/repr is deferred to Plan 102 — so no newtype
// belongs to its carrier's set. But a newtype over `int` invites the reader to
// wonder whether the refusal is about the SET or about the newtype; over `f64`
// there is nothing to wonder about, because even the carrier is not a member.
//
// THE RECEIVER HALF OF #941 IS STILL OPEN, deliberately, and it is not this
// fixture's business: `Meters(2.5).to_i16()` still builds. Its path goes
// through the receiver-resolution site whose result is written into
// `resolved_callees` and therefore changes codegen — widening THAT one was tried
// once and left `d326_value_record_fluent.nv` failing in the C compiler. The two
// halves cost differently and close separately; the row says so.
//
// Spec: D310 (`spec/decisions/02-types.md`, "Type-set bounds").
module neg.p941_ctor_arg_bound_neg

type Meters f64

fn only_ints[T Ints](x T) -> int => 0

fn pass_a_constructor_call_straight_into_a_bounded_parameter() Io -> () {
    // nova:expect E_TYPE_NOT_IN_SET -- `Meters` is not a member of `Ints`; the same value through a binding was already refused, this spelling was not
    println(only_ints(Meters(2.5)))
}
